            (
            %a{:href => "#LewisMaySteinberger86"}> Lewis-May-Steinberger 86, prop. II 9.12
            )
          %h3#AsInversionOfEulerClasses Via inversion of Euler classes
          %p
            We discuss an explicit formula (Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GeometricFixedPointCohomologtIsColimitOverSmashWithEulerClasses"}
            below, due to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#LewisMaySteinberger86"} Lewis-May-Steinberger 86
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"} cohomology groups
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            in partial geometric fixed point spectra (Prop.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PartialGeometricFixedPoint"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"} cohomology groups
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            in the original spectrum, but with certain “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler+classes"} Euler classes
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} inverted
          %p
            As an application, we show (Example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EquivariantStableCohomotopyOfPointsInNontrivalROGDegree"}
            below) that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+stable+cohomotopy"} equivariant stable cohomotopy
            of the point in certain non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RO%28G%29-degrees"} RO(G)-degrees
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            surjects onto the corresponding partially equivariant stable cohomotopy in degree 0 (the latter being well-understood: given by the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Burnside+ring"} Burnside ring
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Burnside+ring+is+equivariant+stable+cohomotopy+of+the+point"} this Prop
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            A key role in this discussion is played by those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RO%28G%29-degrees"} RO(G)-degrees
            which trivialize when passing to partial fixed points:
          #ROGDegreeWithoutNonTrivialHFixedPoints.num_defn
